\name{OAuthConnection}
\alias{OAuthConnection}
\title{OAuthConnection R6 class}
\description{
A connection lets your Shiny server use the current login to call an API or
supply a token to another R package. It checks that the login and permissions
are still available, so your app does not need to keep its own token cache.
}
\details{
Inside reactive server code, obtain a connection with
\code{connection <- shiny::req(auth$connection())}, where \code{auth} comes from
\code{\link[=oauth_module_server]{oauth_module_server()}}. With \code{\link[=oauth_connections_server]{oauth_connections_server()}}, select a saved
login using \code{auth$connection(connection_id)}.

Use \verb{$access_token()} for another R package or database driver, \verb{$request()} for
an API in the client's \code{resource_bases}, and \verb{$has_scopes()} to check permissions
for optional app features. \verb{$request(refresh = TRUE)} can refresh before
sending. See \code{vignette("external-integrations", package = "shinyOAuth")}
(\href{https://lukakoning.github.io/shinyOAuth/articles/external-integrations.html}{online guide})
for a complete example.
}
\section{Connection lifetime}{

A connection belongs to one login and one Shiny session. Token refresh keeps
the same connection and ID. Logout, replacement or session closure invalidates
the old connection; obtain a new one after the next login. A connection manager
can retain the underlying login across navigation, but a new session gets its
own connection reference.

Methods check the owning Shiny session. \verb{$is_usable()} returns \code{FALSE} when
access is unavailable; request and token methods raise errors.
Permission checks do not prove the API will accept a request. The connection ID
is read-only, is not a credential and cannot be used to authorize a request.
}

\section{Advanced lifecycle behavior}{

Reactive expressions that read \code{connection()}, \verb{$access_token()} or
\verb{$has_scopes()} do not rerun on token refresh alone if the login and permissions
stay the same. Status reads such as \verb{$summary()} may update more often. Reads
do not extend a managed owner's inactivity limit. The manager's \code{touch()}
method records explicit user activity.

Use the module's \code{connection()} for coordinated token acquisition, refresh
and token targets. The \code{\link[=oauth_connection]{oauth_connection()}} wrapper follows a supplied token
reactive across logins and does not support those features.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
# Configure outside server(), using an existing provider:
client <- oauth_client(
  provider, client_id = "registered-app",
  redirect_uri = "https://app.example/callback", scopes = c("read", "write"),
  resource_bases = c(api = "https://api.example/v1"),
  required_scopes = "read"
)
server <- function(input, output, session) {
  auth <- oauth_module_server("auth", client, refresh_proactively = TRUE)
  output[["status"]] <- shiny::renderText({
    connection <- shiny::req(auth[["connection"]]())
    connection[["summary"]]()[["status"]]
  })
  records <- shiny::reactive({
    connection <- shiny::req(auth[["connection"]]())
    response <- httr2::request("https://api.example/v1/records") |>
      httr2::req_auth_bearer_token(connection[["access_token"]]("read")) |>
      httr2::req_perform()
    httr2::resp_body_json(response)
  })
}
}
}
