\if{html}{\out{<a id="method-OAuthConnection-summary"></a>}}
\if{latex}{\out{\hypertarget{method-OAuthConnection-summary}{}}}
\subsection{\code{OAuthConnection$summary()}}{
  Resolve the current connection and return status information without
credentials, identity claims or token extension fields. Raises an error
when called outside the owning session or after that session closes.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{OAuthConnection$summary()}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Details}{
    Managed lifecycle states take precedence: \code{refreshing} means a refresh
claim is in progress, \code{uncertain} requires a new authorization after an
ambiguous refresh outcome, \code{disconnected} means local access was removed,
and \code{unavailable} means the stored credentials could not be restored.
Otherwise token status is evaluated in this order:
\itemize{
\item \code{disconnected}: there is no current token.
\item \code{expiry_unknown}: the token's expiry is unknown.
\item \code{expired}: the token has reached its expiry time.
\item \code{insufficient_scope}: the grant lacks a client-required scope.
\item \code{limited}: required scopes are covered, but some other requested scopes
are absent from the grant.
\item \code{active}: all requested scopes are covered.
}

Scope checks enforce retained target limits and use the token's current
\code{granted_scopes}, which may be
assumed or carried forward when an ordinary OAuth provider omits scope
information. SMART clients require explicit evidence and use semantic
coverage for both connection and operation permissions.
See \link{OAuthToken} for the distinction from verified scope evidence.
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    A named list with the following entries:
\itemize{
\item \code{connection_id}: the reference's character ID.
\item \code{client_label}: the client's application-defined character label.
\item \code{status}: one of the character values listed in this method's details.
\item \code{expires_at}: numeric seconds since the Unix epoch, \code{NA_real_} when
there is no token or its expiry is unknown, or \code{Inf} for a
non-expiring token.
\item \code{resource_ids}: character vector of the client's approved resource IDs.
\item \code{replaces_connection_id}: present on managed replacement authorizations;
identifies the locally ended connection passed to \code{reauthorize()}.
}
  }
}

