\subsection{\code{OAuthConnection$request()}}{
  Resolve the current token and perform an authenticated request within a
named resource base. The connection must be usable, and its current grant
must cover any scopes required for this operation.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{OAuthConnection$request(
  resource_id,
  path = "",
  query = NULL,
  method = "GET",
  required_scopes = character(),
  configure = NULL,
  refresh = FALSE,
  target = NULL,
  min_valid_for = 60
)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{resource_id}}{Single character string naming an entry in the client's
\code{resource_bases}.}
      \item{\code{path}}{Single character string resolved relative to the selected base
directory; \code{""} selects the base itself. Absolute and root-relative URLs,
including pagination links, must remain within the same approved origin
and base path. Dot segments and ambiguous encodings are rejected.}
      \item{\code{query}}{Optional named list of query parameters, or \code{NULL}.}
      \item{\code{method}}{Single HTTP method string, defaulting to \code{"GET"}. \code{TRACE}
and \code{TRACK} are rejected by the resource transport.}
      \item{\code{required_scopes}}{Character vector of scopes required for this
operation, in addition to the client's required scopes. They must have
been requested by the client and be covered by the current grant.
\code{character()} adds no operation-specific scope check.}
      \item{\code{configure}}{Optional function taking an unauthenticated \code{\link[httr2:request]{httr2::request()}}
and returning it with only body and application headers changed. Use
\code{\link[httr2:req_body_json]{httr2::req_body_json()}}, \code{\link[httr2:req_body_form]{httr2::req_body_form()}}, \code{\link[httr2:req_body_raw]{httr2::req_body_raw()}}
and \code{\link[httr2:req_headers]{httr2::req_headers()}}. Set the HTTP method with \code{method} above.
URL, transport policies, authentication and Host headers cannot be changed.
Configuration runs once. Both refresh modes recheck the current
authorization afterward, before sending the request.}
      \item{\code{refresh}}{If \code{TRUE}, obtain a usable token before sending the request.
Default \code{FALSE} uses the current token. This is synchronous and cannot wait
for an async refresh. Failed API calls are not refreshed or replayed;
ordinary HTTP retries are disabled for this call. DPoP nonce handling
remains available. The destination and \code{configure} result are checked
before refresh, and permission is checked again before sending.}
      \item{\code{target}}{Optional declared token-target name. Target clients require
an explicit association through that declaration's \code{resource_ids}.}
      \item{\code{min_valid_for}}{Minimum remaining lifetime when \code{refresh = TRUE},
in seconds. Has the same meaning as on \verb{$access_token()}.}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Details}{
    Uses \code{\link[=perform_resource_req]{perform_resource_req()}} with the configured client for Bearer, DPoP
and mTLS authentication. Redirects are never followed. Transport error
messages are redacted to exclude resource paths, queries and response
bodies. Scope requirements are supplied by the application; they cannot
be inferred from an arbitrary API's HTTP method and path.
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    An \link[httr2:httr2]{httr2} response object. Invalid resources, unusable connections,
insufficient scopes and transport failures raise errors.
  }
}

